 "input": 43584, "output": 188}, "context": {"bytes": {"conversation": 49279, "other": 423, "system": 35563, "tool_definitions": 14397, "tool_results": 31479}, "measure": "json-utf8-bytes", "total_bytes": 131141}, "gateway_set": ["thinking.display"], "web_search": false, "web_searches": 0, "calls": {"Bash": 1}}}, {"id": "a70aa56d-36cb-599e-bffd-2ac55270ccad", "at": 1795649818.0501027, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 18985, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "4b62c14ca3520ccf6862bc4e18a94fc1", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "daf5c689-9cae-5c88-af2a-c75a1affebcd", "at": 1795649818.0537567, "until": 1795649819.6418657, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 18986, "layer": "worker", "category": "execute", "kind": "tool.pending", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "4b62c14ca3520ccf6862bc4e18a94fc1", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": "completed", "label": "execute → completed", "repository": null, "tool_call_id": "toolu_01L46wqbu1odxMU73QZhsEpk", "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "forge api repos/world/story-repo/wiki/page/Outline 2>&1 | python3 -c \" import sys,json,base64,re d=json.load(sys.stdin); t=base64.b64decode(d['content_base64']).decode() for l in t.splitlines(): if re.search(r'venue|page|NeurIPS|appendix|b…", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "forge api repos/world/story-repo/wiki/page/Outline 2>&1 | python3 -c \"\nimport sys,json,base64,re\nd=json.load(sys.stdin); t=base64.b64decode(d['content_base64']).decode()\nfor l in t.splitlines():\n    if re.search(r'venue|page|NeurIPS|appendix|budget|reference',l,re.I): print(l[:400])\n\" | head -20"]], "output": "```console\nPractitioners search prompts/pipelines on a benchmark's items and report the best configuration's score on those same items. That number is the maximum of K noisy estimates, so it can overstate how the deployed configuration performs on fresh items; whether it does, by how much, and whether conclusions drawn from such numbers (tuned beats untuned, tool A beats tool B) survive, is an empirical ques\nNumbering is stable; analysis (experiment-repo) owns methods and validity, this page owns what the paper claims and needs. **Rules in §2.1 are fixed before the matrix is analysed** (this is a study about forking paths); experiment-repo accepts or amends them in `experiment-repo`#4 before analysis, and any later change is recorded as a change with its reason, not applied silently.\n| C1 | **Size.** How far is the naive report (best of B evaluated configs on S, scored on S) from the same config's accuracy on fresh items of the same pool, as a function of n and of the evaluated budget B? | Real matrix: n ∈ {30, 60, 100, 200, 400 (flagged: one S is 27% of the pool)}, B ∈ {5, 20, 100}\\* of the K = 128 declared space (exhaustive B = 128 as a supplementary row), random search; tru\n| C3 | **Searcher and leakage.** Does the gap depend on the search algorithm (random; hill-climb; successive halving/best-arm) at equal evaluation budget, and on items placed in the prompt (demos from S)? | Same S and verification folds across searchers; leakage counterfactual Δ_leak on real cells; formula (k/n)Δ_leak. | Fig 2 (panel) / appendix |\n| C4 | **Decisions.** Do reported-score conclusions (tuned beats untuned; tuned A beats tuned B; significance) have false-positive and flip rates away from nominal, against explicit nulls? Objects, fixed here: *untuned* = one fixed default configuration (fixed before looking at the matrix; estimation noise but no selection); *tuned* = best of B on S, reported on S; *tuned-vs-untuned test* = paired\n| C7 | **Beyond the real matrix.** To the extent its validation checks pass, do gap and coverage curves extend to n > 300 and larger K? | Split-half prediction, within-support extrapolation (fit K≤75, n≤100 → K=150, n=200), mis-specification sweep. | Fig 6 / appendix |\n| C8 | **Real tool.** Does the gap of a real search tool (DSPy-style) at the same n and budget fall within the replay searchers' predictive interval? | Short arm; ≥20–30 S draws required for \"tested\", otherwise reported as an illustration and the criterion as **not tested**. | Tab 3 |\n- **C4 nulls:** (i) synthetic equal-θ worlds fitted to the real item-level structure; (ii) real configurations in the densest 0.02-wide window of full-pool accuracy (window chosen by count; dropped, and said so, if < 20 configurations). Rates per scenario × n ∈ {60, 120, 240} × budget ratio {1, 4}.\n**Venue: NeurIPS 2026 main track (venue-repo): 9 content pages including all figures and tables; references, appendix and the mandatory 16-item checklist are outside the limit; double blind.** Floats take at most ≈2 of the 9 pages: Fig 1–5 (each ≈2.3 in high at 5.5 in width) + Tab 1–2 in the main text, Fig 6 and Tab 3 in the appendix. Everything a claim rests on must be inside the 9 pages (reviewe\n8. **Checklist, reproducibility** (venue's checklist is filled by report from owners' facts).\nMain figures carry one claim each. If an experiment cannot yield a figure that shows its claim clearly, tell story-repo and the claim is reworded or moved to the appendix.\n- **Fig 1 (C1) — headline.** Left: the search→report→verify protocol as a small schematic. Right: reported (naive) vs fresh-item accuracy of the selected configuration against n (curves for B), with the verification-noise floor, between-pool intervals, and the default-configuration and mean-random-configuration fresh accuracy as reference lines. *Reads as*: how large the gap is, how it shrinks wit\n- **Fig 3 (C4) — decisions.** False-positive/flip rate of reported-score conclusions vs n (and budget imbalance) under the null s\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "738ec11e-8d6f-50f8-bdbd-8c0406a561ad", "at": 1795649819.2674963,